== Chapter V

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The main aim of the whole discourse set out — The first main case of conscience stated — What it is to mortify any sin, considered negatively — Not the utter destruction of it in this life — Not disguising it — Not the improvement of any natural disposition — Not diverting it — Not an occasional conquest — Occasional conquests of sin, what they are and when they come: when sin breaks out; in a time of danger or trouble.]

#smallcaps[With] these things laid down first, I come to my main purpose: to handle some questions or practical cases that arise in this business of the mortification of sin in believers.

The first, which is the head of all the rest, and to which they all come back, may be considered as set out in the following proposition:

Suppose a man is a true believer, and yet finds in himself a powerful indwelling sin. It leads him captive to its law, consumes his heart with trouble, perplexes his thoughts, weakens his soul for duties of communion with God, and disquiets him as to peace. Perhaps it also defiles his conscience and exposes him to being hardened by the deceitfulness of sin. What shall he do? What course shall he take and insist on to mortify this sin, lust, disorder, or corruption, to such a degree that, though it is not utterly destroyed, yet in his contest with it he may be enabled to keep up power, strength, and peace in communion with God?

In answer to this important question, I will do these things:

I. Show what it is to mortify any sin, both negatively and positively, so that we are not mistaken in the foundation.

II. Give general directions about the things without which it will be utterly impossible for anyone to get any sin truly and spiritually mortified.

III. Draw out the particular ways in which this is to be done. Throughout, I keep in mind that I am treating not the doctrine of mortification in general, but only the particular case proposed above.

=== I. What It Is to Mortify a Sin

==== 1. Negatively: What It Is Not

(1.) To mortify a sin is not to #emph[kill] it outright, to root it out and destroy it so that it has no hold or home in our hearts at all. That is indeed the aim, but it is not to be accomplished in this life. No man who truly sets himself to mortify a sin aims at anything less than its utter destruction, so that it leaves neither root nor fruit in the heart or the life. He would kill it so completely that it would never move or stir again, never cry or call, seduce or tempt, for all eternity. Its #emph[not being] at all is what he aims at. By the Spirit and grace of Christ a man may indeed win a wonderful success and an outstanding victory over a sin, so that he triumphs over it almost constantly. Yet its utter killing and destruction, so that it no longer exists, is not to be expected in this life. Paul assures us of this: “Not that I have already obtained all this, or have already been made perfect” (Phil 3:12). He was a #emph[choice saint,] a pattern for believers. In faith and love and all the fruit of the Spirit he had no equal in the world, and so he can count himself among the “perfect” (that is, the mature) compared with others (verse 15). Yet he had not “obtained,” he was not “perfect,” but was pressing on. He still had a lowly body, as we do, which must be transformed at last by the great power of Christ (verse 21). Utter victory is what we would like. But God sees that it is best for us to be complete in nothing in ourselves, so that in all things we must be complete in Christ (Col 2:10). And that is best for us.

(2.) I think I hardly need to say that it is not the #emph[disguising] of a sin. When a man gives up the practice of a sin for some outward reasons, men may perhaps look on him as a changed man. But God knows that to his former iniquity he has added cursed hypocrisy, and has found a safer road to hell than he was on before. He has gotten a different heart than he had, one that is more cunning; not a new heart, one that is more holy.

(3.) The mortification of sin does not consist in the improvement of a #emph[quiet, calm] nature. Some men have an advantage from their natural constitution, in that they are not exposed to such violence of unruly passions and turbulent affections as many others are. Let these men cultivate and improve their natural frame and temper by discipline, reflection, and prudence, and they may seem to themselves and others very mortified men, when perhaps their hearts are a standing sink of all abominations. One man is perhaps never troubled so much in all his life with anger and passion, nor troubles others so much, as another is almost every day; and yet the latter has done more to mortify the sin than the former. Let such persons not test their mortification by things to which their natural temper gives no life or vigor. Let them bring themselves to self-denial, unbelief, envy, or some such spiritual sin, and they will get a better view of themselves.

(4.) A sin is not mortified when it is only diverted. Simon Magus gave up his #emph[sorceries] for a while, but the #emph[greed and ambition] that drove him to them remained, and would have found another way to act. So Peter tells him, “I see that you are poisoned by bitterness” (Acts 8:23). In other words: “For all the profession you have made, for all that you have given up your sorceries, your lust is as powerful in you as ever. It is the same lust; only its streams have been turned aside. It now shows itself another way, but it is the same old poison of bitterness.” A man may be aware of a lust and set himself against its outbreaks. He may take care that it does not break out as it used to, while all the time he lets the same corrupt habit vent itself some other way. He is like a man who heals over a running sore and thinks himself cured, while his flesh festers with the same infection and breaks out in another place. This kind of diversion, with the changes that come with it, often happens to men for reasons that have nothing to do with grace. A change in a man’s way of life, his relationships, his interests, or his plans may bring it about. Even the changes that come over a man’s body naturally as he grows older may produce such changes. Old men do not usually go on chasing youthful lusts, though they have never put one of them to death. It is the same with trading one lust for another, leaving the service of one to serve another. A man who exchanges pride for worldliness, sensuality for Pharisaism, or vanity about himself for contempt of others, should not think he has mortified the sin he seems to have left. He has changed his master, but he is a servant still.

(5.) #emph[Occasional conquests] of sin do not amount to mortifying it.

There are two occasions or seasons in which a man who is contending with a sin may seem to himself to have mortified it:

\[1.\] When it has had some sad #emph[outbreak], disturbing his peace, terrifying his conscience, making him dread scandal, and plainly provoking God. This awakens and stirs up all that is in the man. It amazes him and fills him with abhorrence of sin, and of himself for it. It sends him to God, and makes him cry out as for his life, abhor his lust as hell, and set himself against it. The whole man, spiritual and natural, being now awake, sin pulls in its head and does not appear, but lies as if dead before him. It is like a man who has crept up to an army in the night and killed a leading officer. At once the guards wake, men are roused, and a strict search is made for the enemy. In the meantime, until the noise and tumult are over, he hides himself or lies like one who is dead, yet firmly resolved to do the same mischief again at the next opportunity. See how the Corinthians, over the sin among them, mustered themselves to surprise and destroy it (2 Cor 7:11). So it is in a person when his lust has made a breach in his conscience, his quiet, perhaps his reputation, by some outbreak of actual sin. Earnestness, indignation, longing, alarm, and revenge are all set to work about it and against it, and lust is quiet for a season, run down before them. But when the hurry is over and the search is past, the thief appears again alive and is as busy as ever at his work.

\[2.\] In a time of some #emph[judgment], calamity, or pressing affliction. The heart is then taken up with thoughts and plans for escaping the present troubles, fears, and dangers. This, a convinced person concludes, can be done only by giving up sin, which brings peace with God. It is the anger of God in every affliction that stings a convinced person. To be free of it, men at such times resolve against their sins. Sin shall never again have any place in them; they will never again give themselves up to its service. Accordingly sin is quiet, does not stir, and seems to be mortified. This is not because it has received a single wound, but only because the soul has filled its faculties, through which sin should exert itself, with thoughts that do not agree with sin’s movements; and when these thoughts are laid aside, sin returns to its former life and vigor. So those in Ps 78:32–37 are a full example and description of this frame of spirit I speak of: “In spite of all this, they kept on sinning; despite His wonderful works, they did not believe. So He ended their days in futility, and their years in sudden terror. When He slew them, they would seek Him; they returned and searched early for God. And they remembered that God was their Rock, that God Most High was their Redeemer. But they deceived Him with their mouths, and lied to Him with their tongues. Their hearts were disloyal to Him, and they were unfaithful to His covenant.” I do not doubt at all that when they sought, and returned, and searched early for God, they did it with full purpose of heart to give up their sins. It is expressed in the word “returned”: to turn or return to the Lord is to give up sin. They did this “early,” that is, with earnestness and diligence. Yet for all this their sin was unmortified (verses 36, 37). And this is the state of many humblings in days of affliction, and a great deceit in the hearts of believers themselves often lies here.

In these and many other ways poor souls deceive themselves and suppose they have mortified their lusts, when the lusts are alive and mighty, and break out on every occasion, to their disturbance and unrest.
